      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white border-b border-slate-800 pb-2">
        Hybrid Search Fusion Architecture: RRF vs Relative Score Fusion (RSF)
      </h2>
      <p>
        In modern production retrieval, BM25 scores are rarely consumed in isolation. They are fused with dense semantic vector representations (such as OpenAI <code>text-embedding-3-large</code>, Cohere Embed v3, or Voyage-3). Two primary mathematical paradigms govern score combination:
      </p>
      <ol class="space-y-4 text-slate-300 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong>Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)</strong>:
          RRF discards raw score magnitudes entirely, operating strictly over ordinal ranks:
          <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-3 rounded font-mono text-xs text-purple-300 my-2">RRF_Score(d) = SUM_{m \in {bm25, dense}} [ 1 / (k + rank_m(d)) ]</pre>
          Setting $k = 60$ prevents top-ranked outliers from dominating. RRF is immune to score scale divergence between unbounded BM25 scores (typically 0 to 45+) and cosine similarity bounds (-1.0 to +1.0).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Relative Score Fusion (RSF / Convex Combination)</strong>:
          RSF performs min-max normalization on both dense and sparse score distributions before linear interpolation:
          <pre is:raw class="bg-slate-950 p-3 rounded font-mono text-xs text-purple-300 my-2">Final_Score(d) = alpha * Norm(BM25(d)) + (1 - alpha) * Norm(Dense(d))</pre>
          When $k_1$ and $b$ are rigorously calibrated, RSF with $\alpha \in [0.35, 0.45]$ outperforms RRF by +1.8% to +2.4% NDCG@10 because it preserves subtle confidence deltas that rank ordering flattens.
        </li>
      </ol>

